What's in it
training/prep_bigearthnet.py — the important one. Real co-registered Sentinel-1 SAR + Sentinel-2 optical, which is the only thing that fixes your biggest risk: the VQA adapter has never seen radar, and half the ISRO eval set is RISAT SAR. It renders SAR through the exact same dB → Lee filter → percentile stretch path raster_io uses at inference, so the adapter trains on the rendering it will actually be served. Questions are synthesised from CORINE labels, and — critically — each question template is balanced independently. Balancing only the global mix still lets "is there water?" run 90% yes while the overall distribution looks healthy; that's how per-question priors get learned.

training/prep_rsvqa.py — RSVQA-LR/HR. A prescribed benchmark that had no code path at all. Balances per question type (presence questions are yes-skewed, comparison no-skewed; globally they cancel and hide both), and never rebalances the test split, since that's the graded benchmark.

RS-adapted grounding. Instead of fine-tuning GroundingDINO — awkward on a free T4 — this fine-tunes PaliGemma's native detect task, which emits <loc0000>–<loc1023> tokens. Same script, same pipeline, and grounding stops being the one component that fails the domain-adaptation requirement. run_grounding dispatches to the adapted VLM when its adapter is loaded, stock DINO otherwise, and records which under applied_parameters.backend.

modules/report.py — self-contained HTML report, images embedded as base64, opens offline, prints to PDF. Third download button in the app.

README.md — architecture, a requirement-by-requirement mapping to the PS, design notes, and an explicit known-limitations section.

Two subtleties worth knowing
Letterbox transform. Grounding boxes are annotated in the source image's pixel space, but the model sees a letterboxed square canvas. box_to_canvas / box_from_canvas apply the transform both ways. Skipping it trains the model on boxes that don't sit on the objects and yields near-zero IoU with no visible error anywhere.

Coordinate conventions. VRSBench boxes may be absolute pixels, per-mille, percent, or normalised. data_prep.py now detects which and records it in build_report.json; training and eval both apply the divisor. Check that field after building — a wrong guess produces near-zero IoU that reads as a broken model rather than a unit mismatch.

